  {
    "episode_id": 91224,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91224.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "Well, that also said to me because when I was governor, we said what I thought was a challenging but reasonable goal for Florida to be in the top for title, the top 25% of the states of America, we've made progress across the board of elementary to our university systems and achieving that goal. And now in every area we've fallen back, and most we are in the bottom, not the top core child. This has hurt our state enormously in many ways, including the fact that we've had a significant decline in the a per capita income of Floridian in comparison to the per capita income of Americans. And I think that the single most important reason for that decline is that extending a signal to an America that is very interested in education understands that if you're going to compete in a post-industrial society economy, that you've got to have highly educated, motivated people. And we're not sending that signal from Florida. And so we've been losing the very jobs that will most contribute to the well-being of our people. We have got to make this front and center. And I hope that in the legislative race that are taking place between now and November 4, the citizens will challenge the was a representative of the legislature's safety as to what they intend to do to get more education out of the ditch and back on to the high road that it was 20 years ago.",
